course: dictionary
lesson: 14663
title: Preferred: liking one thing more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Preferred is the past form of the verb prefer. To prefer something means to like one thing more than another. «I preferred the blue one to the red one.» That means I liked the blue one more.
explain: As an adjective, preferred means the one that is chosen or liked best. «Please write your preferred name on the form.» Your preferred name is the name you like to be called.
explain: Look at the small words after it. We say preferred to or preferred over: «Sam preferred apples over bananas.» The thing you like more comes first, and the thing you like less comes after the word to or over.
fact: Prefer, prefers, preferred and preferring are all forms of the same verb.
fact: «Preferred» means liked one thing more than another.
fact: «Preferred name» means the name you like to be called.
fact: «Preferred» can also mean wanted but not required, as in a job advert.
fact: In a survey, «Which fruit is preferred?» asks which fruit most people like best.
exercise: «Mia preferred the red pencil to the green one.» What does preferred mean here? | Drew a picture with it | Lost the pencil | *Liked one thing more than another | Was given the pencil for free
exercise: «Which name is your preferred name on the form?» What is a preferred name? | A name you have never heard | A secret name | *The name you like to be called | The name on your birth certificate
exercise: Which word is the past form of prefer? | preferring | prefer | prefers | *preferred
test: «Tom preferred cats over dogs.» Which animal does Tom like more? | dogs | *cats | neither | both the same
test: In a class survey, «Which sport is preferred by most children?» What does the question ask? | *Which sport most children like best | Which sport children have never played | Which sport is the oldest | Which sport is the hardest
exam: «Ella preferred walking to the bus, so she walked to school.» Why did Ella walk? | *Because she liked walking more than taking the bus | Because she had lost her bus pass | Because school was closed | Because the bus was late for the whole week
